{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/994316-sorcerers-and-mages","id":994316,"title":"Sorcerers and Mages","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-10-27T18:36:01-07:00","id":994316,"title":"Sorcerers and Mages","content":"Why aren't sorcerers and pychics used more in mage? They seem like a perfect antithesis to mages and their viewpoints by way of &quot;you see how you want reality to be, we see it how it is&quot;. <br /> <br /> They also present an interesting point on the consensual reality vs static reality argument. if humans don't believe in magic, why is it still available in a form usable by Anyone? Is consensual reality a pipe dream spouted by the traditions? A lie about an easy fix to regain their glory when no such thing exists?<br /> <br /> Not to mention their ability to use counter-spells, a starting sorcerer not particularly focused on countering magic can have four dice of counter-magic to subtract from the successes of any spell(and a starting mage has only up to 3 dice of arete to cast magic with). This makes it seem like sorcerers are the vangaurd enforcing reality as opposed to mages who bend reality.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-27T19:25:42-07:00","id":994343,"title":"","content":"The way I see it, mythic threads exist when a significant minority of Sleepers believe in them and the majority doesn't oppose their existence. That is, there's a difference between not believing in something and actively disbelieving it; and Mythic Threads are what happens when the majority can't be bothered to reject what the minority believes.<br /> <br /> Bringing this back to the topic at hand, humans <i>do</i> believe in magic, even in Technocratic Reality Zones \u2014 at least, <i>enough</i> people believe to make it possible, and not enough people are hostile enough to the notion to make it <i>im</i>possible.<br /> <br /> And if the Earthly Foundations are biased toward a given set of metaphysics, the support for it in the Consensus can be smaller and the opposition to it larger and it will <i>still</i> function.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 10-28-2016, 01:14 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-27T20:47:35-07:00","id":994379,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/994316-sorcerers-and-mages?p=994343#post994343\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The way I see it, mythic threads exist when a significant minority of Sleepers believe in them and the majority doesn't oppose their existence. That is, there's a difference between not believing in something and actively disbelieving it; and Mythic Threads are what happens when the majority can't be bothered to reject what the minority believes.<br /> <br /> Bringing this back to the topic at hand, humans <i>do</i> believe in magic, even in Technocratic Reality Zones \u2014 at least, <i>enough</i> people believe to make it possible, and not enough people are hostile enough to the notion to make it [I]im[/]possible.<br /> <br /> And if the Earthly Foundations are biased toward a given set of metaphysics, the support for it in the Consensus can be smaller and the opposition to it larger and it will <i>still</i> function.</div> </div> </div> </div>Personally, I would go a step further, and say Mythic Threads can be supported just by being known. Myths, legends, folk tales, and fiction keep concepts alive in the public consciousness. And, because people know them and value them (even if they don't think of them as true), a portion of their total emotional and mental attention is invested in them.<br /> <br /> Consider the superhero. In less than a century, comic books and other media have popularized the idea of superhumans and super science. The recent rash of superhero movies have only reinforced the cultural impact of them. This creates Mythic Threads - not because most people believe there truly are people who have these powers or their toys, but because the ideas stick with them. In a way, people <i>want</i> to believe in superheroes, even if their &quot;rational sense&quot; tells them they are just fiction. In comes Mythic Threads, creating a place for superheroes that might not be fully supported by the Consensus, but has a presence within it.<br /> <br /> Sorcery and Psychic powers, in many ways, are just like that. The very ideas of Fortune Telling or Telekinesis have persistent influence on even the &quot;rational&quot; person's mind. The common man - the one who doesn't actually believe - still fuels the Mythic Threads, albeit in a way perhaps less potent than the True Believer.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-27T20:53:03-07:00","id":994384,"title":"","content":"On the difference between Sorcerer and Mage, it comes very much down to those Mythic Threads. A Sorcerer can't usually use any old method (Instrument), and expect to get a result. They need to do research, find a teacher, or experiment until they discover (or rediscover) techniques that work. These, of course, being those Instruments ingrained into the popular consciousness. It's why Sorcerers still use the same tools after centuries; they still work. In this way, though, it means that Sorcerers are ultimately dependent on the Mythic Threads, and a well-established thread of belief, and that of the collective consciousness.<br /> <br /> Mages are the only ones whose capacity for magic can be fueled by the <i>Mage's belief alone</i>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-27T20:56:14-07:00","id":994388,"title":"","content":"The effectiveness of countermagic is quite impressive because the average Sorcerer will have 6 to 8 dice for countermagic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-28T08:22:12-07:00","id":994596,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/994316-sorcerers-and-mages#post994316\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They also present an interesting point on the consensual reality vs static reality argument. if humans don't believe in magic, why is it still available in a form usable by Anyone?</div> </div> </div> </div>I dunno how it is in the World of Darkness, but here in our reality there is large number of people who might not believe in wizards and fireballs, and will mock the idea of 'spells', but still do believe in the supernatural. Zodiac signs, going to fortunetellers, fearing black cats that crossed the street, believing in 'Satan's influence', not uttering some sentences (like that someone might have an accident), numerology, some parts of alternative medicine, laws of attraction, ghosts, Friday 13th and karma. I've talked recently to a guy who said he might believe in demons as 'something out there', but not as demons in flesh with horns and ones in the vein of Demon: The Fallen. A priest told me that magic is against God's mandate, cause it is changing reality to how one would like it.<br /> <br /> Belief in magic is much more common in areas other than USA and Europe. Even in those areas there are many who believe in genuine sorcery <a href=\"http://jezebel.com/5617890/way-too-many-americans-believe-in-witches\" target=\"_blank\">http://jezebel.com/5617890/way-too-m...eve-in-witches</a> . In Romania the priest, when baptizing a newborn, spits behind himself to ward off evil charms. In Africa belief in magic is common. China has Traditional Chinese Medicine and Feng Shui, and Japanese hospitals sometimes skip the number 4 in floor numbers because it is considered ill-fated.<br /> <br /> And then there are outright believers in magic and practitioners - Neo-Pagans, chaos magicians, Wiccans, followers of various Etherite-esque theories (psychotronics), Church of Satan (Supposedly atheistic, but you will find descriptions of spells at the end of the Satanic Bible.), Temple of Set. Some Christians, Muslims and other religions might also believe in wholesale magic and warlocks, except that they see them as followers of evil powers.<br /> <br /> While belief in magic is something that loses confrontation in the open, and has been driven from official settings - especially mainstream media, who often talk about &quot;Such amusing superstitions!&quot; - people still do believe in it in many ways; even though when asked directly they will consider something like the Sphere of Correspondence effects and rotes to be pure fantasy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3254,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","user":"Muad'Dib","date":". 10-28-2016, 03:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-28T16:58:51-07:00","id":994953,"title":"","content":"I should probably elaborate. From the In Character mage view sorcerers pose a an antithesis to mages. From the perspective of a sorcerer, as their powers are perfectly defined in reality, they could easily see themselves as part of a static reality that conflicts with the dynamic reality that mages see. Mages would also see how they can naturally negate their magic and see them as enforcers of the current consensus(like human paradox spirits).<br /> <br /> In Character they'd make perfect opponents as they fight for the same resources(nodes, artifacts of power, the secrets of magic) and their views completely conflict.<br /> <br /> OOC sorcerer magic is easily explained by mythic threads(earthly foundations, reality zones, etc.) but that isn't nearly as interesting in story. It takes away so much mystery.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-29T16:29:20-07:00","id":995421,"title":"","content":"And Sorcerers outnumber Mages, though the exact population numbers are debatable. I always assumed that Mythic Sorcerers numbered around 1:2,500 and that Scientific Sorcerers numbered around 1:2,500. That would make them rare but around 80 times more common than Mages (who are very rare at 1:100,000).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-29T17:11:20-07:00","id":995438,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/994316-sorcerers-and-mages?p=994953#post994953\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I should probably elaborate.<b> From the In Character mage view sorcerers pose a an antithesis to mages</b>. From the perspective of a sorcerer, as their powers are perfectly defined in reality, they could easily see themselves as part of a static reality that conflicts with the dynamic reality that mages see. Mages would also see how they can naturally negate their magic and see them as enforcers of the current consensus(like human paradox spirits).<br /> <br /> In Character they'd make perfect opponents as they fight for the same resources(nodes, artifacts of power, the secrets of magic) and their views completely conflict.<br /> <br /> OOC sorcerer magic is easily explained by mythic threads(earthly foundations, reality zones, etc.) but that isn't nearly as interesting in story. It takes away so much mystery.</div> </div> </div> </div>(See the Bold part)<br /> <br /> Unless you are referring to a *specific* mage (or cabal of mages) holding this view/belief, that statement is skewed to say the least. Sorcerers, as presented, are by no means the &quot;antithesis&quot; of Mages -- not at all. They are simply a different side of the metaphysical coin. As stated in different Mage books over the years, one of the benchmarks for (M)ages doing what they do is that as long as (S)orcerers (and other numinically endowed mortals/beings) can still do what they do, that actually *supports* what most (M)ages do in the long run. (That is, as long as The Consensus continues to support and not overly oppose sorcerers, they act as the baseline/bulwark that actually *supports* what the more &quot;properly&quot; Awakened do.) They aren't the antithesis, at all, any more than the lower organs of the body are &quot;antithetical&quot; to the brain -- the brain (higher self/what is the more proper &quot;us&quot;) is supported by the &quot;lesser&quot; organs beneath it (and it, in turn, helps guide and support the organs in return). Many sorcerers follow the (P)aths (directly or by rote pattern) that many mages left in their wake while expanding (or, admittedly, at times constricting) Reality(tm); just as sorcerers in many cases metaphysically support the more dynamic magic(k) of mages, the ever-expanding workings of the latter provide (over time) further hedge magics for sorcerers to attempt to master (whether mythic, technomystical, or any of a number of other possibilities). Many larger cabals of mystics will often have &quot;more talented&quot;, &quot;natural&quot; witches (Mages) working hand-in-hand with their more &quot;studied, salt-of-the-earth&quot; brethren (Sorcerers, or the Psychically Endowed). This is particularly true, for example, among a number of Verbena, Hermetics and even more so among the Choristers, who routinely interact with more &quot;mundane&quot; faith healers, theurgists, and pseudo-hermetic-cum-religious crusaders. Other groups have similar, if often smaller in ratio, counterparts of Mages and Sorcerers -- <i>it just only been peripherally addressed over the various editions/versions of the game setting over the past 20+ years </i>vs. <i>being a more focused aspect. </i><br /> <br /> An important distinction, to say the least...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have been around here for waaaayyyy too fucking long...","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","name":"Just John, Forever...","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-29T17:45:05-07:00","id":995453,"title":"","content":"Setting the two against each other undermines some of the fundamentals of the game. You CAN do that, but that's not the way it's presented. At all. Mages and sorcerers are depicted as working together where the overall praxis of a Tradition and it's accompanying Foci match up with those Mythic Threads that allow for sorcerers to do what they do do. If you want a Sorcerers=Static=Weaver Minions vs. Mages, it seems like an interesting way to set things up, it's just not the way things have been so far. <br /> <br /> Sorcerers are great everywhere in the WoD but Mage and should probably replace reality-rending wishcrafters. In Mage, Sorcerers are pretty much just those not quite good enough to be Mages but not entirely so dull as to be Sleepers. Effectively, they are just stuck throwing down Rotes that they have to learn like arithmetic tables without the benefit of any latitude or allowance for creativity or going off script. Sorcerers are player pianos, records, music boxes. Mages are musicians.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-29T18:01:32-07:00","id":995458,"title":"","content":"I do not really agree. Scientific Sorcerers are great enemies for Tradition Mages (and can be good enemies for Marauders and Nephandi). I would not mind running a Technocracy game where the PCs are Scientific Sorcerers made under the Equality of All rules who are sent to capture Marauders (for psychiatric treatment) and to kill Nephandi (for obvious reasons).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-30T09:06:14-07:00","id":995661,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/994316-sorcerers-and-mages?p=995458#post995458\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I do not really agree. Scientific Sorcerers are great enemies for Tradition Mages (and can be good enemies for Marauders and Nephandi). I would not mind running a Technocracy game where the PCs are Scientific Sorcerers made under the Equality of All rules who are sent to capture Marauders (for psychiatric treatment) and to kill Nephandi (for obvious reasons).</div> </div> </div> </div>Which is a good recipe for dead Extraordinary Citizens. Or insane. Or corrupted. Or both. Or even all three. (I'll just pop your soul in bottle, add a demon for spice, shake it up and let it sit on a shelf for a subjective 10,000 years of torture. Then drink it. Yum... Alternatively, &quot;Jubilee spritz seven Jorgensen? Blue agnostic fripple... Tzatzoloeatl grammar fetish! #@Nq`&amp;xckt#())^!!!&quot; &lt;Poof, wherein the Extraordinary Citizen disappears in a puff of alternate reverse-quantum-spin logic... or becomes &quot;God&quot; for certain values of &quot;God-ness&quot;. As if there were a difference...&gt;)<br /> <br /> Honestly, there are better games out there if that's the set-up you want to play. Any modern iteration of Call of Cthulhu (particularly Delta Green or the Laundry version with a humor-ectomy) with minimal to no tweaking will do it much better.<br /> <br /> And they are great opponent for full-on Traditions mages for about three minutes. When their plan comes into contact with the enemy who starts riffing on reality like a jazz musician while the Technos are playing chop-sticks, Twinkle Twinkle Little Star and Frere Jacques again and again and again. Unless they are taking out blissfully oblivious singleton mages, cabals of initiates or cabals of disciples in a very very bad way due to recent circumstances. And then, with a lot of prep, and mostly undetected, which is nigh impossible when even baby mages are geared up with massive detection abilities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1062,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","user":"Ajax","date":". 10-30-2016, 04:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-30T22:42:55-07:00","id":995968,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Just John, Forever...</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/994316-sorcerers-and-mages?p=995438#post995438\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Many larger cabals of mystics will often have &quot;more talented&quot;, &quot;natural&quot; witches (Mages) working hand-in-hand with their more &quot;studied, salt-of-the-earth&quot; brethren (Sorcerers, or the Psychically Endowed). This is particularly true, for example, among a number of Verbena, Hermetics and even more so among the Choristers, who routinely interact with more &quot;mundane&quot; faith healers, theurgists, and pseudo-hermetic-cum-religious crusaders. Other groups have similar, if often smaller in ratio, counterparts of Mages and Sorcerers -- <i>it just only been peripherally addressed over the various editions/versions of the game setting over the past 20+ years </i>vs. <i>being a more focused aspect. </i></div> </div> </div> </div>To expand on the above, the Order of Hermes, the Verbana, and the Choristers have a heavy base of linear practitioners. Not just as hangers-on, but as a sizable part of their membership.<br /> <br /> The Order of Hermes teaches their initiates Paths of Sorcery as a matter of course. Whether those initiates Awaken or not is immaterial, as both maintain a place in the Order's hierarchy. Granted, the Order has had a long history of bias against their Sorcerer base - there's an impression that if they don't Awaken, they are somehow inferior. And this is a problem that the Tradition as a whole has wrestled with, for good and for ill. The Revised era - where many of the old Masters were lost - set up the possibility for Sorcerers to leverage their collective political might to gain greater respect. After all, the Sorcerers may be asleep, but they are still Hermetics, and that makes them entitled to respect from their fellow wizards.<br /> <br /> Plus, they are also the ones who often do the administrative jobs. And you never want to piss off the bureaucracy. They can make life for their Awakened &quot;betters&quot; a living hell, without needing to rely on spells.<br /> <br /> The Verbanae, on the other hand, has an entire faction - the Gardeners of the Tree - revolving around long family lines and traditional rites. They are the ones who keep the Old Ways, and chart the family trees. And when the Awakening doesn't (usually) work on a hereditary basis, the only way for these families to pass their knowledge on (in lieu of carefully preserving old books, oral tales, and magic items) is to teach Paths of Sorcery from one generation to the next. Any given family member Awakening is considered a good thing, but not the ultimate point, next to which all linear family members are merely stepping stones. The Old Ways as a Paradigm is an end unto itself, whether those arts are preserved by a Sorcerer or taken to new heights by a Mage. Indeed, the Gardeners value innovation much less than preservation. You need to talk to the Moon-Seekers if you want to innovate.<br /> <br /> The Celestial Chorus is, at its core, a religious community (well, more like a series of loosely allied religious communities). Anyone who wishes to serve the One (in whatever guise pleases them) is a valuable member of the Tradition. As are the members with True Faith (assuming you interpret the Celestial Chorus as having both True Magick and True Faith, of course), and even those with no Numina whatsoever. Everyone has a place in the divine order, and everyone has a part of God's plan. If some of those people should show the capacity to work miracles, that doesn't devalue the work that the more humble members do. Indeed, the Sorcerer's actions - or even the non-powered Chorister's actions - can end up being more integral to God's Plan than the miracle worker. God moves in mysterious ways, after all.<br /> <br /> One could easily argue that Chorister Mages must guard themselves from the hubris of thinking themselves better than their &quot;lessers&quot;. The faith healer working to make the lives of a small community better doesn't need to worry about Paradox, you remember, and what is Paradox to a religious Mage but the sign of the One's displeasure? Vulgar magics used to be called Vain...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":6156,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","user":"Bluecho","date":". 10-31-2016, 01:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-31T10:31:47-07:00","id":996138,"title":"","content":"I think that you might be mistaking how mages actually see the world. You seem to be understanding that mages understand what we call the &quot;Purple Paradigm,&quot; which is to say the belief in the malleability of reality and the effect of the Consensus. In effect, metaknowledge. For the vast majority, let's say 99.9% repeating, this is not true. Each mage faction, and by extension, each mage, believes that they've found some way to do the supernatural, but within rules as they understand them. Magick works for Hermetics because they say the right words and draw the right symbols. Magick works for Choiristers because they say the right prayers and sing the right songs. Magick works for Etherites because they built the right machines in the right way according to the right SCIENCE. You get what I mean. What's important to note, however, is that they very rarely believe that they're all doing the same thing. In fact, most of the time, each faction in Mage believes that they're doing something different than the others! Magick to a Hermetic is something completely different from the magick of a Verbena witch, in their own minds, despite the fact that they very much are not.<br /> <br /> This plays into what Sorcerers do. Sorcerers use the methods that mages have developed, methods that have made themselves into the Consensus somewhat. This is why the Paths are so limited in scope, because they've been well defined in what they can do by the belief of those who practice them. It makes them reliable and teachable to the non-awakened. In this way, Sorcery actually reinforces the belief systems of mages since they have &quot;proof&quot; that the methods used by sorcerers (that were developed by mages in the first place) are viable to the non-Awakened. This is also why Disbelief is a thing in Sorcerer: the Consensus affects them too.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6384-frozenfishy","name":"Frozenfishy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"172"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-31T16:17:45-07:00","id":996315,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Frozenfishy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/994316-sorcerers-and-mages?p=996138#post996138\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think that you might be mistaking how mages actually see the world. You seem to be understanding that mages understand what we call the &quot;Purple Paradigm,&quot; which is to say the belief in the malleability of reality and the effect of the Consensus. In effect, metaknowledge. For the vast majority, let's say 99.9% repeating, this is not true. Each mage faction, and by extension, each mage, believes that they've found some way to do the supernatural, but within rules as they understand them. Magick works for Hermetics because they say the right words and draw the right symbols. Magick works for Choiristers because they say the right prayers and sing the right songs. Magick works for Etherites because they built the right machines in the right way according to the right SCIENCE. You get what I mean. What's important to note, however, is that they very rarely believe that they're all doing the same thing. In fact, most of the time, each faction in Mage believes that they're doing something different than the others! Magick to a Hermetic is something completely different from the magick of a Verbena witch, in their own minds, despite the fact that they very much are not.</div> </div> </div> </div>I find this point internally conflicting. The entire war between the traditions and technocracy is partially based on the fact that they each want control of the consensus and acknowledge that magic and reality are shaped by belief. If mages are trapped within their own paradigm, how could they acknowledge the consensus or the fact that true magic comes from belief? Why would agree to practicing the nine sphere(and give up their pillars) if they believed they worked magic differently?<br /> <br /> I choose to just see it as mages know how magic works, they just stick to their their traditions and beliefs for the came reason that first world countries do. A sense of patriotism for their sect.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Frozenfishy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/994316-sorcerers-and-mages?p=996138#post996138\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">This plays into what Sorcerers do. Sorcerers use the methods that mages have developed, methods that have made themselves into the Consensus somewhat. This is why the Paths are so limited in scope, because they've been well defined in what they can do by the belief of those who practice them. It makes them reliable and teachable to the non-awakened. In this way, Sorcery actually reinforces the belief systems of mages since they have &quot;proof&quot; that the methods used by sorcerers (that were developed by mages in the first place) are viable to the non-Awakened. This is also why Disbelief is a thing in Sorcerer: the Consensus affects them too.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Um, I've never read anywhere where it stated that sorcerer magic was a lesser form of mage magic developed by sleepers. I have read that mages have speculated that paths and spheres may have a connection(or that they try to force one), but that's also conflicting with a point that sorcerer revised stressed(me paraphrasing)&quot;Sorcerers are not a lesser form of mages like a ghoul to a vampire. They are their own being that works along side mages&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-31T17:20:44-07:00","id":996334,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/994316-sorcerers-and-mages?p=996315#post996315\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I find this point internally conflicting. The entire war between the traditions and technocracy is partially based on the fact that they each want control of the consensus and acknowledge that magic and reality are shaped by belief. If mages are trapped within their own paradigm, how could they acknowledge the consensus or the fact that true magic comes from belief? Why would agree to practicing the nine sphere(and give up their pillars) if they believed they worked magic differently?<br /> <br /> I choose to just see it as mages know how magic works, they just stick to their their traditions and beliefs for the came reason that first world countries do. A sense of patriotism for their sect.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's really the only way that the Ascension War can make sense without some kind of explicit acknowledgement of the Purple Paradigm by all mages everywhere, which breaks the setting and the system. Also, any mage that believes that their paradigm is the one true paradigm and the only way to perform magick causes some serious dissonance whenever they have to interact with any mage that holds to a different path. The fact that the Consensus is the stakes for the Ascension War does raise some questions, so clearly there is some acknowledgment at some level by some mages that popular belief is important, but it doesn't have to be any more complex than there has been a narrowing of possibilities. Any deeper understandings is reserved for older, more &quot;enlightened&quot; mages who have more time on their hands than the typical protagonist has. <br /> <br /> As for the adoption of the Spheres, in game lore says that it was a contrived method for differing Traditions to be able to talk about magick, but they might consider it differently when talking amongst themselves. It's also how they got the setting to work with the system that the writers developed. The Kopa Loei seem to get away with still ignoring them.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/994316-sorcerers-and-mages?p=996315#post996315\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Um, I've never read anywhere where it stated that sorcerer magic was a lesser form of mage magic developed by sleepers. I have read that mages have speculated that paths and spheres may have a connection(or that they try to force one), but that's also conflicting with a point that sorcerer revised stressed(me paraphrasing)&quot;Sorcerers are not a lesser form of mages like a ghoul to a vampire. They are their own being that works alongside mages&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>That may be true, but then I don't understand why Disbelief affects Linear magick, or how they're related to mages and have mage related merits. It seems to me that sorcerers make more sense as lesser mages, that Paths are using the same mythic threads that mages take advantage of and can be developed by mages who bother codifying what they do, a la the Order of Hermes. It might not be canon, but it's the only way that it makes sense to me if they're going to be included in the Mage gameline.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6384-frozenfishy","name":"Frozenfishy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"172"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-31T18:22:31-07:00","id":996374,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Frozenfishy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/994316-sorcerers-and-mages?p=996334#post996334\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's really the only way that the Ascension War can make sense without some kind of explicit acknowledgement of the Purple Paradigm...<br /> <br /> That may be true, but then I don't understand why Disbelief affects Linear magick, or how they're related to mages and have mage related merits. It seems to me that sorcerers make more sense as lesser mages, that Paths are using the same mythic threads that mages take advantage of and can be developed by mages who bother codifying what they do, a la the Order of Hermes. It might not be canon, but it's the only way that it makes sense to me if they're going to be included in the Mage gameline.</div> </div> </div> </div>I guess the consensus vs. paradigm thing is up to the storyteller.<br /> <br /> The reason for the connection is because the first sorcerer book was a mage flavor book. The only reason they were kept in revised is the same reason some parts of revised's text are copy/pasted from wod:sorcerer without editing, they had serious time and budget constraints that prevented them from doing proper editing. It really Shouldn't be tacked onto mage the way it is, But it's Forced to. Sad really, they could be a great addition to the WOD-verse as a more crossover-friendly game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-31T18:30:08-07:00","id":996379,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/994316-sorcerers-and-mages?p=995968#post995968\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> To expand on the above, (cut, as I'm just wanting to respond w/o re-posting the entire thing)</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, absolutely! (I just glossed over the general references for simplicity's sake, but yeah -- what you added totally drives home the idea of (S)orcerers very often being a very intrinsic part of many (M)age groups, in the broad. Not all, of course, but a sizable ratio depending on the specific group(s) in question...)<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have been around here for waaaayyyy too fucking long...","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","name":"Just John, Forever...","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1218"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}